.Dt XTC_RES 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm xtc_res_init ,
.Nm xtc_res_acquire ,
.Nm xtc_res_release ,
.Nm xtc_res_used ,
.Nm xtc_res_set_cap ,
.Nm xtc_res_high ,
.Nm xtc_res_rejects ,
.Nm xtc_res_set_alert ,
.Nm xtc_res_set_alert_fn ,
.Nm xtc_res_attach_mctx ,
.Nm xtc_res_attach_net
.Nd resource accountant with hard caps and high-water alerts
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In xtc.h
.In xtc_res.h
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_res_init "xtc_res_t *r" "const xtc_res_caps_t *caps"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_res_acquire "xtc_res_t *r" "xtc_res_kind_t k" "int64_t n"
.Ft void
.Fn xtc_res_release "xtc_res_t *r" "xtc_res_kind_t k" "int64_t n"
.Ft int64_t
.Fn xtc_res_used "const xtc_res_t *r" "xtc_res_kind_t k"
.Ft void
.Fn xtc_res_set_cap "xtc_res_t *r" "xtc_res_kind_t k" "int64_t cap"
.Ft int64_t
.Fn xtc_res_high "const xtc_res_t *r" "xtc_res_kind_t k"
.Ft int64_t
.Fn xtc_res_rejects "const xtc_res_t *r" "xtc_res_kind_t k"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_res_set_alert "xtc_res_t *r" "xtc_res_kind_t k" "double pct"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_res_set_alert_fn "xtc_res_t *r" "void (*fn)(xtc_res_kind_t, int64_t, int64_t, void *)" "void *user"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_res_attach_mctx "xtc_res_t *r" "struct xtc_mctx *m"
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_res_attach_net "xtc_res_t *r"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm xtc_res
tracks resource consumption against operator-configured hard caps.
An
.Vt xtc_res_t
is caller-allocated
.Pq typically embedded in an executor or loop
and initialised with
.Fn xtc_res_init ;
it owns no OS resources, so there is no teardown call.
Tracked kinds
.Pq Vt xtc_res_kind_t :
.Bl -tag -width "XTC_RES_MEM_BYTES" -compact -offset indent
.It Dv XTC_RES_TASKS
Live
.Vt xtc_task_t
allocations.
.It Dv XTC_RES_CHANNELS
Live
.Fn xtc_chan_*
objects.
.It Dv XTC_RES_CHAN_SLOTS
In-flight messages across all channels.
.It Dv XTC_RES_FDS
Open file descriptors attributable to xtc
.Pq see Sx WHAT IS METERED .
.It Dv XTC_RES_MEM_BYTES
Bytes in xtc-tracked allocations
.Pq see Sx WHAT IS METERED .
.It Dv XTC_RES_INBOX_MSGS
Cross-loop inbox messages in flight
.Pq per loop, not global .
.El
.Pp
Caps are supplied as an
.Vt xtc_res_caps_t ;
.Dv XTC_RES_CAPS_DEFAULT
gives reasonable values for a multi-loop executor on a workstation.
A
cap of zero means
.Sq no cap
.Pq unbounded; debug only .
The current cap is not readable back through an accessor: it is the
value the caller supplied, and
.Fn xtc_res_set_cap
changes it at runtime.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_res_acquire
charges
.Fa n
units and returns
.Dv XTC_OK ,
or
.Dv XTC_E_RESOURCE
if the request would exceed the cap, or
.Dv XTC_E_INVAL
for a bad kind, a negative
.Fa n ,
or a NULL
.Fa r .
A request that cannot be represented
.Pq Fa n No greater than Dv INT64_MAX No minus the current usage
is rejected with
.Dv XTC_E_RESOURCE
and counted as a reject, rather than being allowed to overflow the
counter: the headroom is tested before the addition, so neither the
hard-cap invariant nor the non-negative accounting invariant can be
broken by a huge request.
.Fn xtc_res_release
gives capacity back, never fails, and clamps at zero on underflow.
Both are atomic and lock-free; safe under concurrent calls.
.Pp
.Sy Alert callbacks :
.Fn xtc_res_set_alert
arms a high-water alert for one kind at
.Fa pct ,
a FRACTION of the cap in
.Pq 0.0 , 1.0 --
so 0.8 means 80 percent, NOT an absolute threshold.
.Fn xtc_res_set_alert_fn
installs the callback
.Pq Fa fn No receiving Pq kind , used , cap , user ;
pass
.Dv NULL
to disable.
The callback fires once when usage crosses the threshold
upward and re-arms when usage drops back below it, so a hovering
workload does not flood.
Alerts are per-kind and independent.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_res_used
reports current usage,
.Fn xtc_res_high
the high-water mark, and
.Fn xtc_res_rejects
the number of acquires rejected because they would have
exceeded the cap.
.Ss WHAT IS METERED
A cap bounds only what is charged against it, and libxtc charges
exactly the following; nothing else is metered:
.Bl -tag -width "XTC_RES_MEM_BYTES"
.It Dv XTC_RES_TASKS , Dv XTC_RES_INBOX_MSGS
Every task spawned on a loop
.Po
.Fn xtc_async ,
.Fn xtc_proc_spawn ,
and their variants
.Pc ,
against that loop's own accountant
.Pq Fn xtc_loop_res ;
a spawn from another thread also charges one inbox message.
.It Dv XTC_RES_CHANNELS , Dv XTC_RES_CHAN_SLOTS
Channels created with a non-NULL accountant, and their in-flight
messages.
.It Dv XTC_RES_MEM_BYTES
.Xr xtc_slab 3
chunks of a cache whose options name an accountant, and
.Xr xtc_mctx 3
chunks
.Pq payload plus the fixed per-chunk header
of a context attached with
.Fn xtc_res_attach_mctx .
.Em Not
.Fn xtc_malloc ,
fiber stacks, process or mailbox structures, or any other internal
allocation.
.It Dv XTC_RES_FDS
Descriptors returned by
.Fn xtc_net_listen ,
.Fn xtc_net_dial ,
.Fn xtc_net_unix_listen ,
.Fn xtc_net_unix_dial
and
.Fn xtc_net_udp_socket
while an accountant is attached with
.Fn xtc_res_attach_net ,
released by
.Fn xtc_net_close .
.Em Not
descriptors from a raw
.Xr accept 2
.Pq xtc_net has no accept call ,
files, pipes, the I/O backend's own descriptors, or
.Xr xtc_xproc 3
control sockets.
.El
.Pp
Metering that needs an attach is off by default: until it is called
nothing is charged, at no cost.
Releases before 1.50 charged
.Dv XTC_RES_MEM_BYTES
for slab chunks only and
.Dv XTC_RES_FDS
for nothing, while this page described both as general.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_res_attach_mctx
charges every chunk of context
.Fa m
to
.Fa r .
The context's current footprint is charged at attach time; if that
alone exceeds the cap the call returns
.Dv XTC_E_RESOURCE
and changes nothing.
Afterwards an allocation that would pass the cap is refused:
.Fn xtc_mctx_alloc ,
.Fn xtc_mctx_calloc
and
.Fn xtc_mctx_strdup
return
.Dv NULL
without touching the heap, and the reject is counted.
.Fn xtc_mctx_free ,
.Fn xtc_mctx_reset
and
.Fn xtc_mctx_destroy
give the bytes back.
Passing a NULL
.Fa r
detaches, releasing the current footprint; attaching elsewhere moves
it.
A child context created while its parent is attached inherits the
accountant; one created earlier does not.
Attach at setup time: it must not race an allocation or free on the
same context.
Returns
.Dv XTC_OK ,
.Dv XTC_E_RESOURCE ,
or
.Dv XTC_E_INVAL
for a NULL
.Fa m .
.Pp
.Fn xtc_res_attach_net
is process-wide.
While
.Fa r
is attached every creating xtc_net call charges one
.Dv XTC_RES_FDS
unit
.Em before
it creates the socket; past the cap it returns
.Dv XTC_E_RESOURCE
and creates nothing
.Pq a refused dial sends nothing .
.Fn xtc_net_close
refunds the unit to whichever accountant the descriptor was charged
to, so a later detach
.Pq Fa r No = NULL
does not unbalance descriptors already open.
Close metered descriptors with
.Fn xtc_net_close :
one closed with a raw
.Xr close 2
keeps its unit until the kernel reuses that descriptor number for
another metered socket.
Always returns
.Dv XTC_OK .
.Pp
This is the centerpiece of xtc's
.Sq predictable behaviour under stress
discipline: every resource that grows under load
must be charged to a res accountant for its cap to hold.
The list above is what the library charges on its own; an
application charges anything else explicitly with
.Fn xtc_res_acquire ,
as in the example below.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Bd -literal -offset indent
xtc_res_t res;
xtc_res_caps_t caps = XTC_RES_CAPS_DEFAULT;
caps.mem_bytes = 100 * 1024 * 1024;       /* 100 MiB */
caps.tasks     = 10000;
caps.fds       = 4096;

xtc_res_init(&res, &caps);
xtc_res_set_alert(&res, XTC_RES_MEM_BYTES, 0.8);   /* 80% of the cap */

/* Charge memory before allocating. */
if (xtc_res_acquire(&res, XTC_RES_MEM_BYTES, sz) != XTC_OK)
    return XTC_E_RESOURCE;
ptr = xtc_malloc(sz);
if (ptr == NULL) {
    xtc_res_release(&res, XTC_RES_MEM_BYTES, sz);
    return XTC_E_NOMEM;
}

/* When done: */
xtc_free(ptr);
xtc_res_release(&res, XTC_RES_MEM_BYTES, sz);
.Ed
